  "response": "Anti-mitochondrial antibody positivity establishes the diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis. Interface hepatitis with a dense plasma cell infiltrate on histology favours autoimmune hepatitis. Eosinophilic infiltration on liver biopsy is pathognomonic for autoimmune hepatitis. A plasma-cell-rich interface lesion that does no more than favour the autoimmune diagnosis and an eosinophil-rich infiltrate that is diagnostic of it on sight cannot both be the way the biopsy speaks to autoimmunity; the two readings of the tissue are mutually exclusive. By contrast with a single antibody result that establishes autoimmune hepatitis outright, the drug attribution is earned longitudinally: improvement of aminotransferases after withdrawal of the suspected agent supports a diagnosis of drug-induced liver injury. The implicated medication was permanently discontinued, and only afterwards was any such fall in aminotransferases recorded; that is, the patient never resumed the implicated medication. Dechallenge, rather than a single marker, therefore carries the weight of the attribution.\n\nBecause the simplified International Autoimmune Hepatitis Group score incorporates autoantibody titres, IgG level, histology, and the absence of viral hepatitis, testing for antinuclear antibody and anti-smooth muscle antibody has become part of the standard workup for suspected autoimmune hepatitis. Because that scoring instrument gives weight to the absence of viral hepatitis, hepatitis A IgM, hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C RNA, and hepatitis E serology are tested to exclude viral causes of jaundice. Only when those assays return negative can the serological and histological components of the score be read at face value.\n\nAn R value of 5 or greater indicates a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. The serum alanine aminotransferase was above the upper limit of normal at presentation, and that enzyme elevation, measured against the threshold, indicates that the injury conformed to the hepatocellular pattern. At least one of the two defining enzymes lay outside its reference range, and perhaps both did: the serum alkaline phosphatase was above the upper limit of normal at presentation. Because the alkaline phosphatase was raised, abdominal ultrasound was performed to exclude biliary obstruction as a cause of jaundice. The pattern so defined frames every later inference about causation.\n\nAdjudication between drug-induced liver injury and autoimmune hepatitis proceeds in stages: the biochemical pattern is characterised, competing causes are excluded, the likelihood of autoimmune disease is scored, and the course after withdrawal of the candidate drug is observed. No single step is decisive, and each is interpreted against the clinical chronology of exposure and jaundice.",
